<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     ARTICLE 1: KNOWING WHAT TO SELL, WHEN, AND TO WHOM
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="article theme-crm" id="art1">
  <div class="article-header" onclick="toggleArticle('art1')">
    <div class="article-header-content">
      <div class="article-num">Article 1 · HBR Tool Kit · March 2006</div>
      <h2>Knowing What to Sell, When, and to Whom</h2>
      <div class="meta">V. Kumar, Rajkumar Venkatesan &amp; Werner Reinartz &nbsp;|&nbsp; University of Connecticut &amp; INSEAD</div>
    </div>
    <span class="article-chevron">▾</span>
  </div>

  <div class="article-body-wrap">
  <div class="article-body">

    <div class="quick-facts">
      <span>CRM Analytics</span><span>Bayesian Estimation</span><span>Purchase Prediction</span>
      <span>Customer Probability Cube</span><span>Marketing ROI</span><span>Overcommunication Effect</span>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Overview &amp; Central Problem <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <p>This article addresses one of the most persistent and costly failures in CRM: despite enormous investments in data infrastructure, most companies remain remarkably bad at predicting what a specific customer will buy, which product they will choose, and — crucially — <em>when</em> they will make that purchase. Traditional CRM-based prediction methods achieve accuracy of only about 60%, barely better than a coin toss. Companies routinely send the wrong offer, to the wrong customer, at the wrong time — wasting resources and actively alienating customers through over-communication.</p>
        <p>The authors argue the root cause is not a failure of CRM data per se, but a fundamental flaw in the <em>mathematical methods</em> used to analyse it. They propose a superior approach derived from Nobel Prize-winning econometric work and demonstrate that it lifts prediction accuracy to approximately 80%, with direct bottom-line impact in the hundreds of millions of dollars.</p>
        <div class="callout">"The secret to achieving a good marketing ROI is simple: Give customers more of what they truly want and less of what they don't."</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">The Traditional Method and Its Fatal Flaws <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <h4>Two-Step Regression Approach</h4>
        <p>The dominant industry practice splits the prediction problem into two separate, sequentially solved regression models: (1) a <strong>Product Choice Model</strong> estimating the probability a customer will choose a particular product, and (2) a <strong>Purchase Timing Model</strong> estimating the probability of a purchase in a given time period. The joint probability is computed by multiplying these two probabilities, producing what the authors call the <strong>Customer Probability Cube</strong> (axes: Customers × Products × Time Periods).</p>
        <h4>Flaw 1: Interdependence Ignored</h4>
        <p>The timing of a purchase is intrinsically linked to the type of product being purchased. Because the traditional method uses two independent regression equations, it completely ignores the interdependence between product choice and purchase timing — so the resulting joint probabilities are systematically wrong.</p>
        <h4>Flaw 2: Sampling Error (The Core Problem)</h4>
        <p>All regression analysis assumes that variable weightings estimated from a sample are representative of the entire population — a statistically untenable assumption. The traditional method has no mechanism to account for or mitigate this sampling uncertainty.</p>
        <div class="framework-box">
          <strong>The Customer Probability Cube</strong>
          <p>A three-dimensional matrix mapping each customer (rows) against each product (columns) and each time period (depth). Each cell contains the probability that Customer <em>i</em> will purchase Product <em>j</em> in Quarter <em>t</em> — enabling highly targeted, resource-efficient campaigns.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">The Proposed Solution: Bayesian Estimation <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <p>The authors build upon the work of Nobel Laureate Daniel McFadden (Economics, 2000) to develop a multivariate <strong>likelihood function</strong> that simultaneously models product choice and purchase timing as a unified, interdependent process.</p>
        <p>Unlike regression analysis, Bayesian estimation: (1) specifies the <em>full range</em> of weightings that could have plausibly generated the observed data, (2) iteratively searches through this range to find the distribution of coefficients most likely to reproduce the actual observed purchasing patterns, and (3) makes predictions that incorporate this uncertainty.</p>
        <p><em>Metaphorically:</em> regression draws the best-fit line through a scatter plot; Bayesian estimation reproduces the actual scatter of dots.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Empirical Validation: Two Company Studies <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>B2B High-Tech Company</th><th>B2C Financial Services</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Sample size</td><td>20,000 customers, 3 years</td><td>10,000 customers, 4 years</td></tr>
            <tr><td>New model accuracy</td><td>74–85% hit rate</td><td>71–89% hit rate</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Traditional model accuracy</td><td>~55% hit rate</td><td>58–65% hit rate</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Profit uplift per customer</td><td>$1,600 avg.</td><td>$400 avg.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>ROI improvement</td><td>160%</td><td>200%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Total profit uplift (full base)</td><td>~$320 million</td><td>~$200 million</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">The Overcommunication Finding <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <p>One of the most counterintuitive findings: the relationship between marketing contact frequency and purchase behaviour is highly <strong>non-linear</strong> — an inverted-U. Below a minimum threshold, communication increases sales. Above an upper threshold, customers experience overload and sales actually decline. Both companies in the study were operating <em>above</em> this upper threshold — actively destroying sales through excessive contact.</p>
        <p>The new model reduced communication costs 31% (B2B) and 26% (financial services) <em>while simultaneously increasing revenue</em>.</p>
        <div class="callout">"Communicating too much can harm you as much as communicating too little. Many companies may be actively damaging their customer revenues in attempts to make sure that no opportunity for a sale is missed."</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Key Concepts &amp; Frameworks <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <div class="concept-grid">
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Customer Probability Cube</strong><p>3D matrix mapping customer × product × time period probabilities for precision targeting.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Bayesian Estimation</strong><p>Models uncertainty in parameter estimates using probability distributions rather than single point estimates.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Sampling Error</strong><p>The primary flaw of regression-based CRM: coefficients estimated on a sample are not reliably representative.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Interpurchase Timing</strong><p>Time intervals between purchases — the dimension where traditional models perform worst.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Communication Threshold Effect</strong><p>Non-linear relationship where both under- and over-communication reduce purchase rates.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>McFadden's Duration Analysis</strong><p>Nobel Prize-winning econometric framework for modelling time-to-event data — the foundation of the authors' likelihood function.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

