<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     ARTICLE 2: MARKETING MYOPIA
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="article theme-crm" id="art2">
  <div class="article-header" onclick="toggleArticle('art2')">
    <div class="article-header-content">
      <div class="article-num">Article 2 · HBR Magazine · Originally 1960, Reprinted 2004</div>
      <h2>Marketing Myopia</h2>
      <div class="meta">Theodore Levitt &nbsp;|&nbsp; Professor Emeritus, Harvard Business School</div>
    </div>
    <span class="article-chevron">▾</span>
  </div>

  <div class="article-body-wrap">
  <div class="article-body">

    <div class="quick-facts">
      <span>Customer vs. Product Orientation</span><span>Business Definition</span>
      <span>Industry Decline</span><span>Self-Deceiving Growth Cycle</span>
      <span>Creative Destruction</span><span>Selling vs. Marketing</span>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Overview &amp; Central Argument <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <p>Published originally in 1960, this is one of the most influential articles in management history. Levitt's audacious claim: <strong>there is no such thing as a growth industry — there are only companies organised to create and capitalise on growth opportunities.</strong> Industries decline because management fails them — specifically, because managers define their business around the <em>product they make</em> rather than the <em>customer needs they serve</em>.</p>
        <div class="callout">"The railroads are in trouble today not because that need was filled by others but because it was not filled by the railroads themselves. They let others take customers away from them because they assumed themselves to be in the railroad business rather than in the transportation business."</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">The Four Conditions of the Self-Deceiving Growth Cycle <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Population Myth:</strong> Belief that growth is assured by an expanding population — breeds complacency and kills creative imagination.</li>
          <li><strong>No Substitute Myth:</strong> Belief there is no competitive substitute — makes firms feel invulnerable and blind to disruptive alternatives.</li>
          <li><strong>Mass Production Trap:</strong> Excessive faith in the advantages of scale — orients the entire organisation inward, away from the market.</li>
          <li><strong>R&amp;D Obsession:</strong> Preoccupation with technical excellence — firms staffed by engineers become product-oriented by default.</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="framework-box">
          <strong>The Self-Deceiving Cycle</strong>
          <p>Early success → attribute success to the product, not the customer need → assume growth is automatic → concentrate investment on production and R&amp;D → customer orientation atrophies → substitutes emerge from outside → industry declines while convinced of its own health.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">The Canonical Examples <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <h4>The Railroads</h4>
        <p>Defined themselves as being in the <em>railroad business</em> rather than the <em>transportation business</em> — so they watched automobiles, trucks, and aircraft capture their customers without mounting any serious competitive response.</p>
        <h4>Hollywood and Television</h4>
        <p>Established film studios defined themselves as being in the <em>movie business</em>, treating television as a mortal threat rather than a new channel for their true purpose: entertainment. Had they been in the <em>entertainment business</em>, television would have been an expansive new opportunity.</p>
        <h4>The Petroleum Industry</h4>
        <p>Defined their business as finding crude oil → refining it → selling gasoline. They never asked: what do motorists actually want? The answer: frictionless personal mobility. They failed repeatedly to capitalise on natural gas, fuel cells, battery vehicles, and solar energy — all being developed by outsiders.</p>
        <h4>Other Vignettes</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Dry Cleaning:</strong> Disrupted by synthetic fibres that required less cleaning — a substitute from a completely different direction.</li>
          <li><strong>Grocery Chains:</strong> Dismissed supermarkets as "cheapy." Those who held their ground were wiped out.</li>
          <li><strong>Buggy Whip Manufacturers:</strong> No product improvement could save the industry once the automobile displaced the horse.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Selling vs. Marketing &amp; The Henry Ford Paradox <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Selling</th><th>Marketing</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Focused on the needs of the <em>seller</em></td><td>Focused on the needs of the <em>buyer</em></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Begins with the product</td><td>Begins with the customer</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Preoccupied with converting product into cash</td><td>Preoccupied with satisfying customer needs through the product</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Marketing is a residual activity</td><td>The entire business is a customer-creating organism</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p><strong>The Henry Ford Paradox:</strong> Ford's genius was marketing, not production. He started with the insight that at $500 per car, millions would buy one. Mass production was the <em>result</em> of his marketing thinking. Ford's eventual failure — insisting on only black cars — was a descent into product orientation, and allowed General Motors to surpass him.</p>
        <div class="callout">"The entire corporation must be viewed as a customer-creating and customer-satisfying organism. Management must think of itself not as producing products but as providing customer-creating value satisfactions."</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Key Concepts &amp; Enduring Relevance <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <div class="concept-grid">
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Marketing Myopia</strong><p>Management's inability to see beyond the current product to the broader customer need — creating strategic blind spots.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Business Definition</strong><p>Narrow definition creates myopia; broad definition around customer need opens growth horizons.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Creative Destruction</strong><p>Firms must proactively obsolete their own products before outsiders do, even when those products are currently profitable.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Population Myth</strong><p>The dangerous assumption that demographic growth automatically sustains industry growth.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Customer Primacy</strong><p>The customer should be the starting point for all business strategy — product is a consequence, not a cause.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Schumpeterian Disruption</strong><p>Levitt's implicit argument: self-disrupt or be disrupted. The 2004 republication is confirmed by Netflix, Amazon, EVs.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

