<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     ARTICLE 3: THE ELEMENTS OF VALUE
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="article theme-crm" id="art3">
  <div class="article-header" onclick="toggleArticle('art3')">
    <div class="article-header-content">
      <div class="article-num">Article 3 · HBR Magazine · September 2016</div>
      <h2>The Elements of Value</h2>
      <div class="meta">Eric Almquist, John Senior &amp; Nicolas Bloch &nbsp;|&nbsp; Bain &amp; Company</div>
    </div>
    <span class="article-chevron">▾</span>
  </div>

  <div class="article-body-wrap">
  <div class="article-body">

    <div class="quick-facts">
      <span>30 Elements of Value</span><span>Value Pyramid</span><span>Maslow's Hierarchy</span>
      <span>Net Promoter Score</span><span>Customer Loyalty</span><span>Revenue Growth</span><span>Discrete Choice Analysis</span>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Overview &amp; Central Argument <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <p>Through three decades of consumer research and a study of 10,000+ U.S. consumers rating nearly 50 companies, the authors identify <strong>30 fundamental "elements of value"</strong> — universal building blocks of what consumers want. These elements are organised into a four-level pyramid (modelled on Maslow) and empirically demonstrated to predict customer loyalty (NPS) and revenue growth.</p>
        <div class="callout">"The right combinations of value elements pay off in stronger customer loyalty, greater consumer willingness to try a particular brand, and sustained revenue growth."</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">The Elements of Value Pyramid <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <div class="pyramid">
          <span class="pyramid-level p1">Social Impact</span>
          <span class="pyramid-level p2">Life Changing</span>
          <span class="pyramid-level p3">Emotional</span>
          <span class="pyramid-level p4">Functional</span>
          <div class="pyramid-caption">Higher levels represent more powerful but rarer forms of value</div>
        </div>
        <h4>Level 1: Functional (Base)</h4>
        <p>Saves time · Simplifies · Makes money · Reduces risk · Organises · Integrates · Connects · Reduces effort · Avoids hassles · Reduces cost · Quality · Variety · Sensory appeal · Informs</p>
        <h4>Level 2: Emotional</h4>
        <p>Reduces anxiety · Rewards me · Nostalgia · Design/Aesthetics · Badge value · Wellness · Therapeutic value · Fun/Entertainment · Attractiveness · Provides access</p>
        <h4>Level 3: Life-Changing</h4>
        <p>Provides hope · Self-actualisation · Motivation · Heirloom · Affiliation &amp; belonging</p>
        <h4>Level 4: Social Impact (Apex)</h4>
        <p>Self-transcendence — enables contribution to something larger than oneself (e.g., TOMS Shoes: buy one, give one)</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Key Empirical Findings <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <h4>More Elements = Dramatically Higher Loyalty</h4>
        <p>Companies scoring high (8+/10) on <strong>four or more elements</strong> achieved <strong>3× the NPS</strong> of companies with only one high score, and <strong>20× the NPS</strong> of companies with no high scores. Apple scored highly on 11 of the 30 elements.</p>
        <h4>More Elements = Faster Revenue Growth</h4>
        <p>Companies with high scores on four or more elements had revenue growth <strong>four times greater</strong> than companies with only one high score.</p>
        <h4>Amazon Prime — Deliberate Value Stacking</h4>
        <ol>
          <li>2005 launch: <em>Reduces cost</em> + <em>Saves time</em> (unlimited 2-day shipping for $79/year)</li>
          <li>Expansion: Added <em>Provides access</em> + <em>Fun/entertainment</em> (Prime Video)</li>
          <li>Further expansion: Added <em>Reduces risk</em> (unlimited photo storage)</li>
          <li>Result: 8 elements; ~40% U.S. retail market penetration; price raised to $99/year</li>
        </ol>
        <h4>The Universal Primacy of Quality</h4>
        <p>Across all industries, <em>perceived quality</em> is the single most powerful predictor of customer advocacy. It is a necessary but not sufficient condition — the entry ticket.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Industry Value Priorities &amp; Digital vs. Omnichannel <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Industry</th><th>Top Value Elements (after Quality)</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Smartphones</td><td>Reduces effort, Variety, Organises, Connects</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Food &amp; Beverages</td><td>Sensory appeal, Variety, Design/Aesthetics, Therapeutic value</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Grocery</td><td>Variety, Sensory appeal, Reduces cost, Rewards me</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Discount Retail</td><td>Variety, Reduces cost, Saves time, Rewards me</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Consumer Banking</td><td>Provides access, Heirloom, Avoids hassles, Reduces anxiety</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Apparel Retail</td><td>Variety, Avoids hassles, Design/Aesthetics, Saves time</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Digital-native companies dramatically outperform on <em>functional</em> elements (saves time, avoids hassles). Brick-and-mortar retailers retain advantages on <em>emotional</em> and life-changing elements — badge value, attractiveness, affiliation — because human interaction delivers emotional value digital channels cannot replicate.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Key Concepts &amp; Frameworks <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <div class="concept-grid">
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Value Stacking</strong><p>Strategically and sequentially adding new value elements to a core offering over time — the Amazon Prime model.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Maslow's Hierarchy (Applied)</strong><p>The theoretical architecture: functional needs first; emotional and life-changing elements differentiate once the functional baseline is met.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Net Promoter Score (NPS)</strong><p>Customer loyalty metric used as the primary outcome variable to validate the model's predictive power.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Laddering Technique</strong><p>Qualitative interviewing method that probes beneath stated preferences to uncover underlying psychological motivations.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Discrete Choice Analysis</strong><p>Customers choose between hypothetical product configurations, revealing the relative importance of each attribute.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Jobs-to-Be-Done (Christensen)</strong><p>Complementary framework — the elements of value provide a structured taxonomy for answering "what job is the customer hiring this product to do?"</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

