<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     ARTICLE 5: WHEN SENSORY MARKETING WORKS AND WHEN IT BACKFIRES
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="article theme-sens" id="art5">
  <div class="article-header" onclick="toggleArticle('art5')">
    <div class="article-header-content">
      <div class="article-num">Article 5 · HBR Digital Article · May 2016 · Reprint H02WHU</div>
      <h2>When Sensory Marketing Works and When it Backfires</h2>
      <div class="meta">Aparna Sundar &amp; Theodore J. Noseworthy &nbsp;|&nbsp; University of Oregon &amp; York University</div>
    </div>
    <span class="article-chevron">▾</span>
  </div>

  <div class="article-body-wrap">
  <div class="article-body">

    <div class="quick-facts">
      <span>Brand Personality</span><span>Sincere vs Exciting Brands</span><span>Sensory Expectations</span>
      <span>Packaging Design</span><span>Sensory Tactics vs Manufacturing</span><span>Consumer Preference</span>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Overview &amp; Central Argument <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <p>Sensory marketing does not work uniformly for all brands. The effectiveness of a sensory tactic is <strong>highly dependent on a brand's perceived personality</strong>. Consumers intuitively link a brand's packaging and marketing tactics to their broader preconceptions of the brand's character — and they reward or punish brands whose sensory signals match or violate those expectations.</p>
        <div class="callout teal-callout">"For your packaging to evoke the right narrative, it must be designed intentionally. Consider how innovation in packaging would communicate to your consumer in the larger scheme of your brand's perceived personality. Above all, remember to design accordingly."</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">The Sincere vs. Exciting Framework <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <div class="brand-personality-split">
          <div class="bp-box bp-sincere">
            <h4>Sincere Brands</h4>
            <p>Brand personality built on <strong>consistency, dependability, and trustworthiness</strong>. Consumers reward sensory tactics that <em>deliver exactly what is expected</em>. Unexpected sensory surprises are punished — even if objectively superior. Examples: <strong>Hallmark, Ford, Coca-Cola, Nokia, L.L. Bean</strong></p>
          </div>
          <div class="bp-box bp-exciting">
            <h4>Exciting Brands</h4>
            <p>Brand personality built on <strong>innovation, energy, and surprise</strong>. Consumers reward sensory tactics that <em>violate expectations</em> and surprise them. Predictable sensory experiences feel flat. Examples: <strong>Mountain Dew, BMW, Pepsi, Apple, Nike</strong></p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="cb-moment" style="border-color:var(--teal);">
          <div class="moment-label" style="color:var(--teal);">Key Finding: Punishment &amp; Reward Effects</div>
          <p>Consumers actually <em>punished</em> sincere brands by expressing less desire to purchase, even when a sensory tactic revealed superior quality (packaging looked plastic but was actually metal). Consumers <em>rewarded</em> exciting brands by expressing greater purchase desire, even when the sensory tactic revealed inferior quality (packaging looked metal but was actually plastic). <strong>The brand expectation, not the objective quality, was what mattered.</strong></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">The Apple vs. Nokia Smartphone Study <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <p>The authors built a new smartphone combining elements of the Apple iPhone 6 and Nokia Lumia 925. They marketed it alternately as an Apple or Nokia product, using each brand's logo. The phone <em>looked</em> plastic but could be made to <em>feel</em> like metal, cardboard fibre, or (as expected) plastic.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Apple (exciting brand):</strong> Consumers wanted the phone more when its promotional bumper case <em>violated</em> their sensory expectations — when assumed plastic but was metal or even cardboard (despite cardboard being inferior).</li>
          <li><strong>Nokia (sincere brand):</strong> Consumers wanted the phone more when its promotional tactic <em>delivered exactly what was expected</em> — a plastic bumper case — despite metal being objectively superior.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="framework-box">
          <strong>Critical Distinction: Tactic vs. Manufacturing</strong>
          <p>This effect only applied when sensory surprise was perceived as a brand <em>tactic</em> (packaging, promotional accessories). When the phone <em>itself</em> was made of different materials, consumers focused on objective quality regardless of brand. The inference that "the brand is choosing to surprise me" was what activated brand personality expectations.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Consumer Behavior Concepts Illustrated <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <div class="cb-moment" style="border-color:var(--teal);">
          <div class="moment-label" style="color:var(--teal);">CB Concept: Brand Schema / Consumer Expectations</div>
          <p>Consumers hold mental schemas about brand personalities. Any sensory input is evaluated against this schema. For sincere brands, schema-consistent cues produce positive affect; schema-violating cues produce negative affect even when the signal is objectively good. This is schema congruity theory applied to sensory marketing.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cb-moment" style="border-color:var(--teal);">
          <div class="moment-label" style="color:var(--teal);">CB Concept: Shared Knowledge &amp; Social Signalling</div>
          <p>Consumer perceptions of brand personality often become shared knowledge — "everyone knows Luna bars are for women." This shared knowledge acts as a social norm that frames whether a sensory tactic feels appropriate or transgressive.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cb-moment" style="border-color:var(--teal);">
          <div class="moment-label" style="color:var(--teal);">CB Concept: Heuristic Processing vs. Systematic Processing</div>
          <p>When evaluating sensory marketing tactics (not manufacturing), consumers switch to heuristic processing — using brand personality as a mental shortcut to evaluate whether a sensory signal is "right for this brand." This explains why inferior material (cardboard) was accepted for Apple: the excitement heuristic overrode objective evaluation.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cb-moment" style="border-color:var(--teal);">
          <div class="moment-label" style="color:var(--teal);">Oreo Application: Where Does Oreo Sit?</div>
          <p>This article directly illuminates the Oreo India dilemma. Oreo's "Twist, Lick, and Dunk" ritual positions it as a <strong>sincere, family-oriented brand</strong> (consistent experience, predictable ritual, warmth). The case study confirms that consumers associated Oreo with <em>sharing and collective consumption</em> — sincere values. Sunfeast Dark Fantasy, by contrast, positioned itself as <strong>exciting and indulgent</strong>. The implication: sensory innovations (new formats, flavours, packaging surprises) work better for Dark Fantasy; consistent sensory ritual execution works better for Oreo. Any sensory tactic Oreo considers must reinforce its family-sharing identity, not violate it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Key Concepts <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <div class="concept-grid">
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Brand Personality</strong><p>The set of human characteristics associated with a brand. Aaker (1997) identified five dimensions: Sincerity, Excitement, Competence, Sophistication, Ruggedness.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Sensory Expectation Violation</strong><p>When a sensory experience doesn't match what the consumer anticipated. Works positively for exciting brands; damages sincere brands.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Schema Congruity</strong><p>The degree to which new information matches a consumer's existing mental model of a brand. High congruity = comfort; low congruity = either delight (exciting brands) or discomfort (sincere brands).</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Tactic Attribution</strong><p>The consumer inference that a sensory experience was deliberately chosen by the brand — as opposed to a neutral manufacturing decision. Only when a tactic is attributed to the brand does personality alignment become important.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Tactile Marketing</strong><p>The deliberate use of touch (texture, weight, resistance, temperature) as a brand communication channel — from Coca-Cola's contoured bottle to Marfa's burlap packaging.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Multi-Sensory Brand Identity</strong><p>The deliberate combination of sight, touch, sound, smell, and taste to create and maintain brand image or personality in a consumer's mind.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

