═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="article theme-case" id="art6">
  <div class="article-header" onclick="toggleArticle('art6')">
    <div class="article-header-content">
      <div class="article-num">Article 6 · IIMB Case Study IMB 519 · 2015</div>
      <h2>Sensory Branding: Oreo in the Indian Context</h2>
      <div class="meta">S Ramesh Kumar, Nalin Goel &amp; Gireesh Gera &nbsp;|&nbsp; Indian Institute of Management Bangalore</div>
    </div>
    <span class="article-chevron">▾</span>
  </div>

  <div class="article-body-wrap">
  <div class="article-body">

    <div class="quick-facts">
      <span>Sensory Branding</span><span>Brand Repositioning</span><span>Functional / Emotional / Symbolic Consumption</span>
      <span>Indian FMCG</span><span>Premium Biscuits</span><span>Consumer Perception</span>
      <span>Twist Lick Dunk</span><span>Brand Personality</span>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section" id="art6-summary">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Overview &amp; Central Problem <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <p>This IIMB case study examines one of the most compelling real-world applications of sensory branding and consumer behavior theory in the Indian FMCG market. It is set in March 2015 — four years after Oreo's India launch — when the brand had achieved the remarkable position of India being its <strong>3rd largest global market</strong>, yet Oreo's market share in the cream biscuit segment had stagnated at a constant <strong>6%</strong> for two years.</p>
        <p>The case centres on a single, high-stakes decision: should Chella Pandyan <strong>reposition Oreo</strong>? And if so, along which dimensions — functional, emotional, or symbolic — given the formidable competition from Sunfeast Dark Fantasy and Britannia Pure Magic?</p>
        <div class="callout blue-callout">The core research questions: How should Oreo differentiate itself from competitors? Should the brand reposition itself after it had created awareness in India? How should consumer perception be taken into consideration? — Chella Pandyan, Country Head (Biscuits), Mondelez India</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Key Characters &amp; Stakeholders <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <div class="character-grid">
          <div class="character-card">
            <div class="char-name">Chella Pandyan</div>
            <div class="char-role">Country Head (Biscuits), Mondelez India Foods Private Limited</div>
            <p>The central decision-maker of the case. He is the strategic protagonist who must navigate Oreo's stagnant 6% market share against deeply entrenched Indian competitors. He commissions both the 62-respondent survey and the six-person focus group, and must interpret their results to formulate a repositioning strategy. His dilemma is quintessentially a consumer behavior problem: how to shift perception without alienating the brand's established identity.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="character-card">
            <div class="char-name">S Ramesh Kumar</div>
            <div class="char-role">Professor of Marketing, IIMB — Case Author</div>
            <p>Brings the conceptual scaffolding of sensory branding, functional/emotional/symbolic consumption dimensions, and brand perception theory to the analysis of Oreo's situation in India.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="character-card">
            <div class="char-name">The 62 Survey Respondents</div>
            <div class="char-role">Young adults (18–26), well-educated, post-graduate</div>
            <p>The voice of the target consumer. They had seen and/or consumed all three brands. They scored each brand on 20 statements across functional, emotional, and symbolic dimensions on a 5-point Likert scale — producing the quantitative evidence Chella Pandyan needed.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="character-card">
            <div class="char-name">The 6 Focus Group Participants</div>
            <div class="char-role">Subset of the 62 — qualitative insights</div>
            <p>Their discussion revealed rich qualitative findings: the guilt of consuming calorie-dense biscuits, the inability to stop mid-pack, the pride of serving premium cream biscuits to guests, and critically — the perception that Oreo = sharing/collective consumption, while Dark Fantasy and Pure Magic = solitary self-indulgence.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="character-card">
            <div class="char-name">Ranbir Kapoor</div>
            <div class="char-role">Bollywood Celebrity — Oreo Brand Ambassador (India)</div>
            <p>Cast as a fun, youthful, aspirational figure to tap the young-adult market. His Air Force pilot advertisement (the "Ranbir and his sister" storyboard) embeds the Oreo brand in middle-class family playfulness and emotional bonding — reinforcing the sincere, relationship-oriented brand personality that differentiates Oreo from the more sensual/solitary positioning of its competitors.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="character-card">
            <div class="char-name">The Beautiful Lady in the Castle</div>
            <div class="char-role">Dark Fantasy Advertisement Character</div>
            <p>Not a real person, but the central character in the Sunfeast Dark Fantasy ad. She represents luxury, exclusivity, materialism, and sensual indulgence — seen in a satin dressing gown, surrounded by diamonds and exotic pets, eating the biscuit in a sensual manner. She embodies the "escape into one" fantasy that positions Dark Fantasy as solitary, aspirational, and hedonically exciting.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">The Indian Biscuits Market: Context &amp; Stakes <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Total Indian biscuit market (2014)</td><td>INR 240 billion</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Premium cookie + cream segment</td><td>INR 72 billion (30% of total)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Expected growth rate of premium segment</td><td>17% per annum</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Profit margin advantage (premium vs. value)</td><td>~2.5× higher</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Britannia — cookie market share</td><td>~30%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Parle — combined cookie+cream share</td><td>~25%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>ITC (Sunfeast) — cream biscuit share</td><td>~26%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Oreo (Cadbury/Mondelez) — cream biscuit share</td><td>~6%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>The premium segment had replaced glucose biscuits as the biggest segment in the biscuit industry — a dramatic structural shift driven by post-liberalisation consumer lifestyle changes and rising middle-class aspirations for indulgence.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Brand Profiles: Oreo, Dark Fantasy &amp; Pure Magic <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <div class="brand-compare">
          <div class="brand-card oreo">
            <div class="b-name">Cadbury Oreo</div>
            <p style="color:#333;font-size:0.82rem;line-height:1.6;">
              Launched: March 2011 (India)<br>
              Parent: Mondelez (formerly Kraft)<br>
              Target: Children, teens; young adults 25–40<br>
              Position: Global aspirational brand, Western origin, family bonding<br>
              Ritual: "Twist, Lick, and Dunk"<br>
              Ambassador: Ranbir Kapoor<br>
              Price: INR 5–10 for small packs<br>
              Tagline: Implied — "Family Fun"<br>
              Key sensory cue: Distinctive embossed cookie design, dark chocolate + sweet vanilla contrast
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="brand-card dark">
            <div class="b-name">Sunfeast Dark Fantasy</div>
            <p style="line-height:1.6;font-size:0.82rem;">
              Parent: ITC Foods (since 2003)<br>
              Target: Teens + young adults 15–25<br>
              Position: Ultra-premium, sensual indulgence, "escape"<br>
              Tagline: "Pure Indulgence" → "Escape into One"<br>
              Packaging: Ultra-premium matte finish, rich colours, calligraphic script<br>
              Key sensory cue: Molten chocolate cream, aromatic cocoa &amp; vanilla, rich tactile packaging<br>
              FB following: 1.6 million subscribers
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="brand-card magic">
            <div class="b-name">Britannia Pure Magic</div>
            <p style="color:#333;font-size:0.82rem;line-height:1.6;">
              Parent: Britannia Industries (since 1892)<br>
              Nationally present since 1989; relaunched 2012<br>
              Target: "Neo-rich, self-indulgent, worldly-wise" young adults<br>
              Position: Fine chocolate artistry, luxury, excess, greed<br>
              Tagline: "Pure Bliss, Pure Magic"<br>
              Variants: Vanilla crème, chocolate crème, praline crème<br>
              Price: INR 25 for 100g pack<br>
              Key sensory cue: Gooey cream, liquid chocolate imagery, visual premium packaging
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Brand Communication Analysis — The Three Storyboards (Exhibit 2) <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <p>The three advertisements reveal starkly different sensory and emotional strategies — each a distinct application of consumer behavior theory:</p>

        <div class="cb-moment">
          <div class="moment-label">Oreo — Ranbir Kapoor &amp; His Sister (Air Force Pilot Ad)</div>
          <p><strong>Setting:</strong> Middle-class Indian home. Ranbir as an Air Force pilot about to leave for duty. Sister says she'll miss him.<br>
          <strong>Key Dialogue:</strong> Ranbir says have Oreo whenever you miss me — "it has chocolatey sweet cream, just like me; and a crunchy cookie, just like me." When she asks how Oreo is like <em>her</em>, he teases: "Oreo's crunchy wrapper is like you — chatting all day long."<br>
          <strong>CB Concepts:</strong> Affective appeal; emotional bonding; brand as relationship metaphor; playfulness and love in sibling relationships. The product attributes (chocolate, crunch) are mapped onto human personality traits — a powerful form of brand anthropomorphism. The middle-class setting grounds the aspirational Western brand in relatable Indian emotional contexts.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cb-moment">
          <div class="moment-label">Sunfeast Dark Fantasy — The Lady in the Castle</div>
          <p><strong>Setting:</strong> Two parallel imageries: a beautiful lady in a faraway castle with diamonds, jewellery, exotic pets, satin dressing gown; and molten cream and chocolate flowing like a river, biscuits falling into molten chocolate, white cream oozing between cookies.<br>
          <strong>Key Voiceover:</strong> "How far will you go for your dark fantasy? Sunfeast Dark Fantasy – Escape into one."<br>
          <strong>CB Concepts:</strong> Aspirational imagery; materialism; hedonism; sensory indulgence; self-pampering; escapism. The food porn imagery of flowing chocolate creates what the case calls "affective appeal to one's sensory receptors of the body, urging the consumer to revel in that sweet sin." This is classic hedonic consumption framing — the biscuit as a guilt-laden, sensual escape.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cb-moment">
          <div class="moment-label">Britannia Pure Magic — The Liquid Cream Showcase</div>
          <p><strong>Setting:</strong> Pure product imagery. Strands of liquid cream and chocolate being mixed and sandwiched between chocolate cookies. Biscuits falling into melted chocolate. No human characters.<br>
          <strong>Key Voiceover:</strong> "Pure Crème, Pure Chocolate. Voila. Pure Magic."<br>
          <strong>CB Concepts:</strong> Sensory appeal through visual food imagery; sophistication signalling; premium positioning through product-only advertising (no narrative distraction). The absence of characters makes the product itself the character — aligning with the brand's "fine chocolate artistry" positioning and appealing to sensory hunger directly.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Consumer Perception Survey — Exhibit 1 Analysis <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <p>62 respondents (age 18–26, mostly post-graduates) scored each brand on 20 statements on a 5-point Likert scale. Key findings:</p>

        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Statement</th><th>Oreo</th><th>Dark Fantasy</th><th>Pure Magic</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Provides me energy (functional)</td><td>2.82</td><td>2.79</td><td>2.74</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Comfort food — makes me happy</td><td>3.52</td><td class="score-high">3.63</td><td>3.48</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Associated with expensiveness</td><td>3.34</td><td class="score-high">4.18</td><td>3.73</td></tr>
            <tr class="score-high"><td>I can identify with this brand</td><td class="score-highlight">3.35</td><td>3.16</td><td>2.94</td></tr>
            <tr class="score-high"><td>I consume &amp; share with friends</td><td class="score-highlight">3.45</td><td>3.34</td><td>3.23</td></tr>
            <tr class="score-high"><td>I consume as a quick snack</td><td class="score-highlight">3.50</td><td>3.39</td><td>3.34</td></tr>
            <tr class="score-high"><td>I consume to satisfy hunger pangs</td><td class="score-highlight">3.42</td><td>3.29</td><td>3.19</td></tr>
            <tr><td>I feel like treating myself</td><td>3.27</td><td class="score-high">3.56</td><td>3.18</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Consumed only by specific type of people</td><td>3.05</td><td class="score-high">3.47</td><td>3.15</td></tr>
            <tr><td>These biscuits are indulgent</td><td>3.63</td><td class="score-high">4.11</td><td>3.71</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Made by careful processing &amp; handling</td><td class="score-high">4.05</td><td>4.00</td><td>3.87</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Very creamy</td><td>3.87</td><td class="score-high">4.03</td><td>3.66</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Communicates who I am to others</td><td>3.27</td><td>3.23</td><td>2.97</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Have a great taste</td><td>4.13</td><td class="score-high">4.16</td><td>3.77</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Very crispy cookie</td><td class="score-high">3.85</td><td>3.82</td><td>3.66</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Very pleasing appearance</td><td>3.98</td><td class="score-high">4.23</td><td>3.74</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Wonderful aroma</td><td>3.45</td><td class="score-high">3.77</td><td>3.40</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Excellent quality</td><td>4.19</td><td class="score-high">4.27</td><td>3.92</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Helps me become the person I want to be</td><td>2.47</td><td>2.34</td><td>2.21</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Induces strong feelings &amp; sentiments</td><td>2.82</td><td>2.77</td><td>2.63</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="callout blue-callout">
          <strong>Key Interpretation:</strong> Dark Fantasy dominates on aspirational/indulgent/sensory dimensions (expensiveness: 4.18, indulgence: 4.11, appearance: 4.23, aroma: 3.77). Oreo leads on functional and sharing dimensions (quick snack: 3.50, hunger satisfaction: 3.42, sharing with friends: 3.45, brand identification: 3.35). Pure Magic consistently scores lowest on most dimensions — the weakest brand perception of the three.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Focus Group Findings — Rich Qualitative Insights <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <div class="cb-moment">
          <div class="moment-label">CB Concept: Guilt-Based Consumption / Hedonic Restraint</div>
          <p>Participants pointed out a <strong>guilt feeling attached to the consumption</strong> of premium cream biscuits. They knew the biscuits were sugary and calorie-dense and hence could not be consumed daily. This exemplifies the tension between hedonic and utilitarian motivations — consumers desired the sensory indulgence but were restrained by health consciousness and caloric awareness.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cb-moment">
          <div class="moment-label">CB Concept: Loss of Control / Impulsive Consumption</div>
          <p>Participants mentioned it was difficult to fight their urges once a pack was opened — they felt compelled to finish it in one sitting, <strong>adding to the guilt</strong>. This illustrates the concept of impulse control failure under hedonic stimuli and the "cookie monster" effect — once the consumption ritual begins, the hedonic momentum is hard to interrupt.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cb-moment">
          <div class="moment-label">CB Concept: Social Signalling &amp; Status Consumption</div>
          <p>Some participants felt they could now <strong>proudly serve premium cream biscuits to their guests</strong> — previously they would buy from local bakeries. This reveals how premium FMCG products serve as social currency. The branded premium biscuit communicates taste, sophistication, and caring to guests — a form of impression management and identity expression through consumption.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cb-moment">
          <div class="moment-label">CB Concept: Collective vs. Solitary Consumption Modes</div>
          <p>Most significant finding: participants linked <strong>Oreo to sharing between friends and collective consumption</strong>, while they linked <strong>Dark Fantasy and Pure Magic to solitary self-pampering</strong>. This is a fundamental perceptual divide — Oreo occupies the social consumption space; its competitors occupy the private indulgence space. These are entirely different jobs-to-be-done and different moments of consumption.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cb-moment">
          <div class="moment-label">CB Concept: Consumption Occasion Confusion</div>
          <p>Some respondents were <strong>confused as to when to consume such biscuits</strong>. Unlike glucose or Marie biscuits (daily functional snack), premium cream biscuits had no clear consumption occasion in their daily routines. This suggests a category-level problem with occasion definition — an opportunity for Oreo to own a specific consumption occasion (e.g., "share with someone you care about").</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Consumer Behavior Concepts Highlighted Throughout the Case <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <div class="concept-grid">
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Sensory Branding</strong><p>Using multi-sensory cues (taste, visual, tactile, aroma) to create distinctive brand perceptions — the core theme. Oreo's embossed design, dark chocolate cookie, sweet vanilla cream, and tactile "twist" ritual are all sensory branding elements.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Functional Consumption</strong><p>The degree to which a brand satisfies basic needs — hunger, convenience, portable snacking. Oreo scores higher than competitors on these dimensions in the survey.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Emotional Consumption</strong><p>The degree to which consumption generates psychological states — comfort, happiness, self-reward, excitement. The affective appeal of the Twist, Lick, and Dunk ritual creates emotional value beyond nutrition.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Symbolic / Social Consumption</strong><p>The degree to which consumption signals identity, status, or group membership. Dark Fantasy scores highest on expensiveness (4.18) and "consumed only by specific people" (3.47) — stronger symbolic consumption cues.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Brand Ritual</strong><p>"Twist, Lick, and Dunk" is a deliberately engineered consumption ritual — a sequential, sensory procedure that transforms the act of eating a biscuit into an experience. Rituals create strong habitual associations and differentiate the experience from competitors.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Country of Origin Effect</strong><p>Oreo's American origin creates an aspirational "Western appeal" that influences perception among Indian consumers — reflecting the globalisation of consumer preferences and the aspirational pull of Western brand heritage.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Impulse Buying</strong><p>Smaller SKUs for Oreo were designed to facilitate sampling and impulse purchase — a key behavioural driver in the premium cookie segment. Low price point of entry reduces perceived risk for trial.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Brand Perception Mapping</strong><p>The 20-item Likert survey is a structured brand perceptual mapping exercise across three consumption dimensions — allowing competitive positioning to be made explicit and measurable.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Repositioning Strategy</strong><p>The challenge of shifting a brand's position in consumer perception without alienating existing users. Oreo must consider whether to deepen its functional/sharing positioning or move toward the indulgence space occupied by Dark Fantasy.</p></div>
          <div class="concept-box"><strong>Hedonic vs. Utilitarian Motivation</strong><p>The tension between the desire for sensory pleasure (hedonic) and functional need satisfaction (utilitarian) drives all three brands' communication strategies differently — and shapes which consumer occasions they can realistically own.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Oreo's Sensory Branding Toolkit: Applied Analysis <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <h4>Visual Sense</h4>
        <p>The <strong>enigmatic embossed design</strong> on every Oreo cookie is one of the most recognisable tactile-visual brand marks in the world. The stark black-and-white contrast (dark chocolate cookie, white cream) creates instant visual distinctiveness on shelf. The blue packaging with the "OREO" logo in milk-splash imagery signals freshness, indulgence, and the Twist-Lick-Dunk ritual opportunity.</p>
        <h4>Taste Sense</h4>
        <p>Oreo deliberately retained the original <strong>bitter chocolate cookie with sweet vanilla cream</strong> — a tension-driven taste profile that creates sensory interest through contrast. This decision was strategic: to maintain differentiation from local Indian brands and to meet the aspirational expectation of the "real" Western product. The bittersweet contrast drives repeat consumption and craving.</p>
        <h4>Tactile Sense</h4>
        <p>The <strong>Twist, Lick, and Dunk ritual</strong> is a masterpiece of tactile brand engineering. It requires physical engagement — twisting apart the biscuit (tactile resistance), licking the cream (taste and touch), dunking in milk (visual and sound). This three-step tactile sequence creates a consumption experience that competitors cannot replicate — it is a brand-owned ritual, not just a product attribute.</p>
        <h4>Aroma &amp; Sound</h4>
        <p>The crispy <strong>crunch of the Oreo cookie</strong> (scoring 3.85/5 for "very crispy cookie") contributes an auditory sensory cue that reinforces perceived freshness and quality. The aroma of chocolate and vanilla (3.45/5 for aroma, slightly below Dark Fantasy's 3.77) represents an area where Oreo could potentially strengthen its sensory positioning.</p>

        <div class="framework-box">
          <strong>The Sensory Hierarchy for Oreo India</strong>
          <p><strong>Strong:</strong> Visual distinctiveness (emboss, packaging), Tactile ritual (Twist-Lick-Dunk), Taste contrast (bitter-sweet). <strong>Moderate:</strong> Cookie crunch (3.85), aroma (3.45). <strong>Weaker vs. Competitors:</strong> Indulgence perception (3.63 vs. Dark Fantasy's 4.11), aspirational imagery, premium sensory appearance (3.98 vs. Dark Fantasy's 4.23). <strong>Strategic Gap:</strong> Oreo leads on functional ritual consumption but trails on premium sensory indulgence — the fastest-growing value in the category.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section" id="art6-analysis">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Cross-Article Connections: Theory in Action <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <div class="cb-moment">
          <div class="moment-label">Connection to Article 4: The Science of Sensory Marketing</div>
          <p>The Oreo case is a live instance of every principle in the HBR sensory marketing article. The "Twist, Lick, and Dunk" ritual is analogous to the Hershey's Kiss foil-unwrapping — a designed tactile sequence that creates hedonic value. The crisp crunch of the cookie corresponds to the BMW engine sound — engineered auditory cues that signal quality. The case explicitly investigates whether brands in the premium biscuit category "only serve the functional needs of satisfying hunger or was there anything more" — exactly the question Aradhna Krishna's research illuminates.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cb-moment">
          <div class="moment-label">Connection to Article 5: When Sensory Marketing Backfires</div>
          <p>The Sundar &amp; Noseworthy framework directly explains the competitive dynamic: Oreo is a <strong>sincere brand</strong> (family, warmth, ritual, sharing — consistent sensory experience). Dark Fantasy is an <strong>exciting brand</strong> (sensual, escapist, indulgent — surprise and excess are rewarded). This explains why focus group participants linked Oreo to collective consumption and Dark Fantasy to solitary pampering — they have unconsciously categorised each brand by personality type. For Oreo's repositioning, this framework warns: do not introduce sensory surprises or violations of the Twist-Lick-Dunk ritual; instead, deepen the consistency and warmth of the existing sensory identity.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cb-moment">
          <div class="moment-label">Connection to Article 3: Elements of Value</div>
          <p>Mapping the survey data onto the Elements of Value framework reveals that Oreo scores strongly on <em>functional</em> elements (saves time: quick snack 3.50, hunger satisfaction 3.42, quality 4.19) and moderately on <em>emotional</em> elements (comfort food: 3.52, affiliation/sharing: 3.45). Dark Fantasy dominates on <em>emotional</em> elements (therapeutic: indulgence 4.11, sensory appeal 4.23, badge value: consumed by specific people 3.47). To improve NPS and loyalty, the Almquist framework prescribes that Oreo needs to strengthen its score on <em>more</em> elements — particularly moving from 3 strong elements to 5+.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cb-moment">
          <div class="moment-label">Connection to Article 2: Marketing Myopia (Levitt)</div>
          <p>Applying Levitt's lens: Oreo must not define itself as being in the "cream biscuit business." It is in the <em>joyful sharing rituals business</em>. The moment it defines its product too narrowly (dark chocolate + vanilla cream in a sandwich cookie), it becomes vulnerable to any competitor who can replicate the product attributes. Defining the business around the customer need — "I want to share a playful, fun ritual with someone I care about" — opens differentiation avenues that go far beyond the biscuit itself: new rituals, new occasions, co-branded experiences, digital interactions around the TLD (Twist-Lick-Dunk) moment.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="collapsible-section" id="art6-discussion">
      <div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection(this)">Discussion Questions for the Classroom <span class="section-chevron">▾</span></div>
      <div class="section-body">
        <ol>
          <li>The survey data shows Oreo leads on functional and sharing dimensions while Dark Fantasy leads on indulgence and sensory appeal. Should Oreo double down on its existing strengths or attempt to compete on Dark Fantasy's turf? What are the risks of each approach?</li>
          <li>The focus group reveals that consumers perceive Oreo as a "collective consumption" brand. Is this a constraint to be overcome or a differentiating asset to be amplified? How does the Sundar &amp; Noseworthy framework inform your answer?</li>
          <li>Using the Elements of Value pyramid (Almquist et al.), map Oreo's current value profile across the 30 elements. Which new elements could Oreo credibly add without violating its brand personality?</li>
          <li>The "Twist, Lick, and Dunk" ritual is a designed sensory sequence. How does it compare to Hershey's Kiss foil-unwrapping and BMW's engine sound amplification as examples of engineered sensory brand value? What could Oreo do to deepen the ritual?</li>
          <li>Given Levitt's Marketing Myopia framework, how should Oreo define the business it is in? What adjacent categories or occasions does this definition open up?</li>
